After completing Auto Scaling Groups (ASG) hands-on, you should now be able to confidently answer both interview and AWS SAA questions like these.

Basic Questions
1. What is an Auto Scaling Group?

An Auto Scaling Group is an AWS service that automatically launches, terminates, and replaces EC2 instances to maintain the desired number of healthy instances.

2. Why do we need Auto Scaling?
High availability
Fault tolerance
Cost optimization
Automatic scaling based on demand
3. What are the three capacities in an ASG?
Minimum Capacity
Desired Capacity
Maximum Capacity

Example:

Minimum = 2
Desired = 4
Maximum = 8
4. What happens if an EC2 instance crashes?

The ASG detects the unhealthy instance through health checks, terminates it, and launches a replacement automatically.

5. What happens if you manually terminate an EC2 instance inside an ASG?

The ASG notices that the number of running instances is below the desired capacity and launches a new instance automatically.

You actually observed this behavior in your lab.

Launch Template Questions
6. What is a Launch Template?

It stores the configuration for launching EC2 instances, such as:

AMI
Instance type
Security Group
Key Pair
User Data
IAM Role
7. Why does an ASG need a Launch Template?

Because the ASG needs a blueprint to know how to create replacement instances.

Health Check Questions
8. What health checks does an ASG use?
EC2 Status Checks
Elastic Load Balancer (ALB/NLB) Health Checks
9. Difference between EC2 health checks and ALB health checks?

EC2 Health Check

Checks whether the virtual machine itself is healthy.

ALB Health Check

Checks whether the application running on the instance is responding correctly.
Scaling Questions
10. What is Scale Out?

Adding more EC2 instances when demand increases.

11. What is Scale In?

Removing EC2 instances when demand decreases.

12. Name the scaling policies.
Target Tracking Scaling
Step Scaling
Simple Scaling
Scheduled Scaling
Predictive Scaling
Load Balancer Integration
13. Why do we use an ALB with an ASG?

The ALB distributes incoming traffic across multiple healthy EC2 instances, while the ASG maintains the correct number of healthy instances.

14. What is a Target Group?

A Target Group is a collection of backend resources (usually EC2 instances) that receive traffic from a Load Balancer.

15. What happens when a target becomes unhealthy?

The Load Balancer stops sending traffic to it, and if the ASG uses ELB health checks, the ASG replaces the instance.

User Data
16. What is User Data?

A startup script that runs automatically the first time an EC2 instance boots.

17. Why did we use User Data?

To automatically:

Install Apache
Create the HTML page
Configure the web server

This ensured every replacement instance was configured without manual work.

Availability Questions
18. Why should an ASG span multiple Availability Zones?

If one Availability Zone fails, instances in the other zone continue serving traffic, improving availability.

19. Can an ASG exist without a Load Balancer?

Yes. It can maintain the desired number of instances without a Load Balancer, but clients would need another way to reach those instances.

20. Can an ASG replace unhealthy instances automatically?

Yes.

That is one of its core features.

Scenario-Based Questions
21. Your website receives 10× more traffic. What happens?

If scaling policies are configured, the ASG launches additional EC2 instances, and the Load Balancer distributes traffic among them.

22. One EC2 instance stops responding. What happens?
The Load Balancer health check fails.
The instance is marked unhealthy.
The ASG terminates it.
A new instance is launched.
The new instance registers with the Target Group after becoming healthy.
23. Why is Desired Capacity important?

It defines how many instances the ASG tries to keep running at all times.

24. Can you update software on every future EC2 instance automatically?

Yes. Update the User Data or create a new version of the Launch Template, then have the ASG use that version for new instances.